==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 09 — NETCAT PART 2
Listener & Simple File Transfer
==========================================

--- SHELL-STYLE LISTENER (educational, LOCAL ONLY) ---
nc -lvnp 4444                       # listener waiting for a connection
nc 127.0.0.1 4444 -e /bin/bash       # pipe a shell into the connection (if -e is supported)
                                     # many modern nc builds disable -e on purpose (abuse risk)

--- SIMPLE FILE TRANSFER ---
nc -lvnp 4444 > received_file.txt    # receiver: save whatever comes in to a file
nc 127.0.0.1 4444 < test_file.txt     # sender: pipe a file's contents into the connection

--- BASIC TWO-WAY CHAT / PIPE TEST ---
nc -lvnp 5555                        # listener
nc 127.0.0.1 5555                     # connect, then type messages back and forth

--- WHY THIS MATTERS FOR SECURITY ---
A netcat listener + a piped shell is the same basic mechanism behind a "reverse shell" -
one of the most common post-exploitation techniques. Understanding it defensively means
recognizing: unexpected outbound connections, unusual listening ports, and processes
spawning shells unexpectedly (all things covered in earlier Linux journal days: ss -tuln,
ps aux, auth.log analysis).

